k, Wren's courage against the Thorn Witch's spite, a brave mouse's loyalty against everything. Light, it turns out, is very stubborn."}, {'n': 4, 'title': 'Starlight Restored', 'text': "the Briar Vale celebrated: the fireflies spelled Wren's name, the oak dropped acorns of thanks, the wise owl composed a ballad with a brave mouse as the hero — a brave mouse insisted Wren was the hero; they compromised on both.\n\na silver acorn chose to stay with Wren — or Wren chose to stay with it; by now they weren't sure there was a difference. It sits by the bed, glowing softly, a nightlight of pure story.\n\nThey carried the starlight home to the Briar Vale in a silver acorn, which had grown to hold it all. The brook ran forward again. The mushrooms glowed gold. The stars came back, one by one, like friends returning."}, {'n': 5, 'title': 'The Promise Kept', 'text': "Wren grew, as heroes do, but never grew out of the Briar Vale. Every spring they check the old oak. Every night a silver acorn glows. Every tale a brave mouse tells starts, 'When we saved the stars...'\n\na brave mouse is older now, greyer, no less brave. They still walk together, still argue about the ballad, still keep the grumble-jar dusted. Friendship, like starlight, doesn't fade if you tend it.\n\nbe brave, be kind. In the Briar Vale, in the starlight, in every heart that hears the tale: the promise kept, the story told, the magic real."}, {'n': 6, 'title': 'Bedtime', 'text': "be brave, be kind. Goodnight.\n\nWren smiles from the doorway. Outside, the stars are out — all of them, every one, back where they belong. The brook sings. The fireflies dance.\n\nAnd now it's bedtime in the Briar Vale, and a silver acorn glows soft, and a brave mouse is telling the story one more time, to one more sleepy listener. 'The end,' a brave mouse says."}]}

